WEBVTT

00:00.270 --> 00:02.720
- [Voiceover] "At time t is
greater than or equal to zero,

00:02.720 --> 00:05.550
"a particle moving along
a curve in the xy-plane

00:05.550 --> 00:09.290
"has position x of t and y of t."

00:09.290 --> 00:11.460
So its coordinate is given
by the parametric function

00:11.460 --> 00:14.640
x of t, and y-coordinate by the
parametric function, y of t.

00:14.640 --> 00:17.990
"With velocity vector v of t is equal to,"

00:17.990 --> 00:20.090
and the x component of the velocity vector

00:20.090 --> 00:21.730
is cosine of t squared,

00:21.730 --> 00:23.570
and the y component of the velocity vector

00:23.570 --> 00:25.920
is e to the 0.5t.

00:25.920 --> 00:27.660
"At t equals one,

00:27.660 --> 00:32.198
"the particle is at
the point 3, comma, 5."

00:32.198 --> 00:34.200
Is at the point 3, comma, five.

00:34.200 --> 00:35.387
All right.

00:35.387 --> 00:39.467
"Find the x-coordinate of
the position of the particle

00:39.467 --> 00:43.683
"at time t is equal to two."

00:43.683 --> 00:45.709
All right, so how do we think about this?

00:45.709 --> 00:48.640
Well, you could view the
x-coordinate at time t equals two.

00:48.640 --> 00:52.600
So let's say, so we
could say x at time two,

00:52.600 --> 00:56.276
which they don't give that to us directly,

00:56.276 --> 01:01.270
but we could say that's
going to be x of one

01:01.270 --> 01:04.540
plus some change in x

01:04.540 --> 01:08.130
as we go from t equals
one to t equals two.

01:08.130 --> 01:10.218
But what is this going to be?

01:10.218 --> 01:12.920
Well, we know what the velocity is.

01:12.920 --> 01:15.500
And so the velocity,
especially the x component,

01:15.500 --> 01:17.180
and we can really focus on the x component

01:17.180 --> 01:18.740
for this first part
'cause we only wanna know

01:18.740 --> 01:21.472
the x-coordinate of the
position of the particle.

01:21.472 --> 01:26.010
Well, we know the x component of velocity

01:26.010 --> 01:27.160
as a function of t

01:27.160 --> 01:32.160
is cosine of t squared.

01:32.160 --> 01:35.000
And if you take your velocity
in a certain dimension

01:35.000 --> 01:38.737
and then multiply it times
a very small change in time,

01:38.737 --> 01:41.800
in a very small change in time, dt,

01:41.800 --> 01:44.450
this would give you your
very small change in x.

01:44.450 --> 01:46.610
If you multiply velocity
times change in time,

01:46.610 --> 01:50.092
it'll give you a displacement.

01:50.092 --> 01:53.000
But what we can do is we can
sum up all of the changes

01:53.000 --> 01:56.930
in time from t equals one to t equals two.

01:56.930 --> 01:58.855
Remember, this is the change in x

01:58.855 --> 02:02.510
from t equals one

02:02.510 --> 02:05.115
to t is equal to two.

02:05.115 --> 02:06.570
So what we have right over here,

02:06.570 --> 02:08.160
we can say that x of two,

02:08.160 --> 02:10.013
which is what we're trying to solve,

02:10.013 --> 02:11.860
is going to be x of one,

02:11.860 --> 02:14.160
and they give that at time equals one,

02:14.160 --> 02:16.600
the particle is at the
point three, comma, five.

02:16.600 --> 02:18.454
Its x-coordinate is three.

02:18.454 --> 02:20.950
So this right over here is three.

02:20.950 --> 02:23.930
And then our change in x from t equals one

02:23.930 --> 02:25.940
to t equals two is going
to be this integral.

02:25.940 --> 02:29.540
The integral from t
equals one to t equal two

02:29.540 --> 02:33.420
of cosine of t squared, dt.

02:33.420 --> 02:35.330
And just to make sure we
understand what's going on here,

02:35.330 --> 02:38.800
remember, how much are we
moving over a very small dt?

02:38.800 --> 02:41.000
Well, you take your
velocity in that dimension

02:41.000 --> 02:43.800
times dt, it'll give you a
displacement in that dimension,

02:43.800 --> 02:46.030
and then we sum 'em all
up from t equals one

02:46.030 --> 02:48.022
to t equals two.

02:48.022 --> 02:49.730
And in this part of the AP test,

02:49.730 --> 02:51.360
we're allowed to use calculators

02:51.360 --> 02:53.494
and so let's use one.

02:53.494 --> 02:54.902
All right.

02:54.902 --> 02:57.808
So there's my calculator.

02:57.808 --> 03:00.590
And I can evaluate.

03:00.590 --> 03:03.192
So let's see, I wanna evaluate three

03:03.192 --> 03:05.050
plus the definite integral.

03:05.050 --> 03:06.470
I click on Math,

03:06.470 --> 03:08.732
and then I can scroll down

03:08.732 --> 03:11.680
to function integral, right there.

03:11.680 --> 03:13.865
The definite integral of,

03:13.865 --> 03:15.790
and I make sure I'm in radian mode,

03:15.790 --> 03:18.172
that's what you should assume,

03:18.172 --> 03:19.900
unless they tell you otherwise.

03:19.900 --> 03:23.050
Cosine of t squared, now
I'll use x as my variable

03:23.050 --> 03:25.900
of integration, so I'll say cosine of x,

03:25.900 --> 03:28.707
cosine of x squared.

03:28.707 --> 03:31.370
And then my variable of integration is x,

03:31.370 --> 03:33.290
so I'm really integrating
of x squared, dx,

03:33.290 --> 03:35.280
but it'll give the same value,

03:35.280 --> 03:38.515
comma, from one

03:38.515 --> 03:40.903
until two,

03:40.903 --> 03:44.390
and now let the calculator
munch on it a little bit,

03:44.390 --> 03:49.207
and I get approximately 2.557.

03:49.207 --> 03:51.950
So this is approximately

03:51.950 --> 03:55.541
2.55,

03:55.541 --> 03:58.197
2.557.

03:58.197 --> 04:00.620
Let me make sure that I added the three.

04:00.620 --> 04:03.180
Yeah, three plus that definite integral

04:03.180 --> 04:04.040
from one to two.

04:04.040 --> 04:06.810
2.557.

04:06.810 --> 04:07.920
And I just rounded that.

04:07.920 --> 04:09.040
So there you go.
